# Mount command automation coverage

Status: planned

Related live specifications:

- `tests/automation/ordinary_widget_mount_live_spec.lua`
- `tests/automation/overlay_widget_mount_live_spec.lua`
- `tests/automation/complete_screen_mount_live_spec.lua`
- `tests/automation/native_screen_mount_live_spec.lua`
- `tests/automation/interaction_live_spec.lua`

## Purpose

Provide direct live-DFHack evidence that every applicable public DwarfSpec
command works through each distinct mount host and native subject source.
Existing unit coverage remains responsible for exhaustive argument validation
and injected failure combinations; this checklist covers behavior that depends
on real DFHack mounting, screen routing, input dispatch, rendering, geometry,
or cleanup.

This checklist is the implementation completion contract. A phase is complete
only when every task and exit-gate item in that phase is checked and its fresh
evidence is recorded in the evidence ledger.

## Scope

Included mount hosts:

- ordinary `gui.View` widget components;
- `plugins.overlay.OverlayWidget` components;
- complete `gui.ZScreen` components;
- non-owning native-screen attachments.

Included native-mount subject sources:

- `ESubjectSource.NATIVE`;
- `ESubjectSource.OVERLAY`.

Included command surfaces:

- top-level `ds` commands;
- equivalent fluent `Subject` methods;
- supported overloads and mount-specific rejection behavior;
- exact unmount and test-fixture cleanup.

Excluded:

- duplicating every unit-test argument permutation in live automation;
- tests of VS Code tasks, package manifests, or static configuration contents;
- adding production commands solely to simplify test coverage;
- relying on unstable world data when a reversible test-owned fixture can prove
  the same routing contract;
- treating a command returning without error as proof when an observable result
  is available;
- changing runner transport, scheduling, result persistence, or the separate
  in-game runner UI.

## Coverage model

Each command is tested once for behavior shared entirely above the mount
boundary. It is repeated for a mount host or subject source when that boundary
changes any of:

- interaction target;
- subject adapter;
- geometry;
- render observation;
- screen ownership;
- input fall-through;
- cleanup responsibility;
- supported versus rejected behavior.

Run-level commands `ds.current_run()`, `ds.wait_frames()`, `ds.await()`, and
`ds.stage_overlay_registration()` already have live coverage and are not
repeated for every mount host.

## Locked acceptance contract

☐ Every supported mount host has direct live coverage for its applicable
  command routes.
☐ Every supported native subject source has direct live coverage for lookup,
  inspection, interaction, rendering, capture, staleness, and cleanup.
☐ Top-level and fluent command forms are both exercised where they enter
  meaningfully different resolution paths.
☐ Expected unsupported behavior is asserted as an explicit error with no state
  mutation.
☐ Pointer and mouse tests place the pointer over a rendered target before
  dispatch.
☐ Keyboard, text, click, and wheel tests prove an observable result through
  normal DFHack dispatch.
☐ Redraw tests distinguish default waiting from `{wait=false}`.
☐ Screen captures and view-tree captures validate bounded returned content, not
  merely that a table was returned.
☐ Live tests never depend on execution order or state retained by another test.
☐ Every test-owned screen, overlay registration, configuration change, pointer
  claim, and instrumentation hook is restored independently.
☐ Native-screen tests never dismiss or take ownership of the borrowed game
  screen.
☐ A passing functional assertion never substitutes for independent cleanup
  evidence.

## Global engineering rules

☐ Add LuaDoc comments to every new or changed Lua function, method, class, and
  class-like test fixture.
☐ Reuse public DwarfSpec commands in acceptance tests; do not invoke native
  widget callbacks or overlay `onInput()` methods directly.
☐ Prefer test-owned reversible screens and overlays over mutating persistent
  fortress state.
☐ Preserve the actual native screen, focus, stack, pause state, viewport,
  pointer, button state, overlay configuration, and render dispatcher.
☐ Use bounded waits with explicit descriptions for asynchronous observations.
☐ Avoid arbitrary frame delays when an observable completion condition exists.
☐ Keep shared test helpers under `tests/support` or
  `tests/automation/support`; do not add test-only helpers to the release rock.
☐ Do not add packaging-contract or VS Code task-configuration tests.
☐ Preserve unrelated worktree changes.
☐ Record focused live, full live, unit, syntax, diff, process-state, and cleanup
  evidence separately.

## Phase 0: Baseline and authoritative coverage matrix

Goal: establish the exact public command and mount capability inventory before
adding tests.

### Tasks

☐ Verify repository path, branch, remote, and initial `git status --short`.
☐ Enumerate the current public top-level commands from `src/ds.d.lua` and
  `src/dwarfspec/ds.lua`.
☐ Enumerate the current fluent `Subject` methods from `src/ds.d.lua` and
  `src/dwarfspec/subject.lua`.
☐ Record the four mount hosts and the two native subject sources.
☐ Create an applicability matrix containing every command surface and classify
  each cell as:
  - already proven live;
  - missing live coverage;
  - shared behavior proven by another cell;
  - explicitly unsupported;
  - blocked by unavailable stable live state.
☐ Link every already-proven cell to its exact automation specification and
  assertion.
☐ Link unit-only behavior to its existing unit specification without treating
  it as live proof.
☐ Record baseline `.\tools\Run-UnitTests.ps1` output.
☐ Record baseline `.\tools\Check-Lua.ps1` output.
☐ Verify a responsive DFHack process and record its PID, executable, current
  viewscreen type, focus, and loaded-world preconditions.
☐ Run `.\tools\Run-AutomationTests.ps1` and record the baseline full-live result
  and cleanup confirmation.

### Phase 0 exit gate

☐ Every public command and fluent method appears exactly once in the
  applicability matrix.
☐ Every missing case maps to a later task in this checklist.
☐ Shared and unsupported classifications include a concrete reason.
☐ Baseline unit, syntax, full-live, process, and cleanup evidence is recorded.

## Phase 1: Shared live conformance support

Goal: make the missing cases concise, deterministic, and consistent without
hiding mount-specific behavior.

### Tasks

☐ Add a shared command-conformance helper only where it removes duplicated
  setup or assertions across at least two mount hosts.
☐ Keep mount construction in each mount-specific specification so the actual
  host category remains visible.
☐ Define explicit capability fields for:
  - root inspection;
  - tree capture;
  - screen capture;
  - subject pointer placement and hover;
  - keyboard input;
  - text input;
  - physical mouse states;
  - default-wait redraw;
  - no-wait redraw;
  - viewport support or expected rejection.
☐ Reject unknown or contradictory capability fields in the helper.
☐ Provide reusable assertions for exact mounted-root identity.
☐ Provide reusable assertions for default-wait and no-wait redraw generation
  behavior.
☐ Provide reusable pointer/button snapshot and exact-restoration assertions.
☐ Provide reusable bounded capture assertions.
☐ Provide reusable command-observation counters that are owned by the test
  fixture and reached only through normal input/render dispatch.
☐ Ensure helper cleanup runs after both successful assertions and injected
  failures.
☐ Add focused unit coverage for nontrivial helper validation or cleanup logic;
  do not add unit tests that merely restate static fixture tables.

### Phase 1 exit gate

☐ The helper contains no mount implementation or production behavior.
☐ Mount-specific tests still show which real host and interaction target are
  exercised.
☐ Shared cleanup assertions fail when any owned state remains.
☐ Focused helper tests and Lua syntax checks pass.

## Phase 2: Ordinary-widget command gaps

Goal: close the remaining live command gaps for the ordinary-widget host.

### Tasks

☐ Add a rendered ordinary-widget pointer target that records ordinary host
  input dispatch.
☐ Place the pointer inside that target before every physical mouse action.
☐ Exercise `ds.mouseInput(EMouseButton.LEFT)` and prove the default click is
  observed.
☐ Exercise explicit `EInputState.DOWN` and `EInputState.UP` and prove temporary
  button fields and final restoration.
☐ Exercise wheel input and prove handled or normal fall-through behavior
  explicitly.
☐ Exercise root-default `ds.inspect()` and validate the ordinary component
  root identity, visibility, activity, and bounds.
☐ Exercise top-level `ds.redraw()` with default waiting.
☐ Exercise `ds.redraw(nil, {wait=false})`, prove it returns before the next
  render generation, then separately await completion.
☐ Verify pointer, button, viewport, pause, host-screen, and instrumentation
  restoration after unmount.

### Phase 2 exit gate

☐ Every previously missing ordinary-widget command case has an observable live
  assertion.
☐ Physical mouse states and no-wait redraw do not leak state.
☐ Existing ordinary-widget automation remains green.

## Phase 3: Overlay-widget command gaps

Goal: close the remaining live command gaps for an owned overlay-widget mount.

### Tasks

☐ Assert `ds.root():raw()` is the exact mounted overlay instance.
☐ Add a test-owned overlay keyboard target and prove both `ds.input()` and
  `Subject:input()` use normal overlay dispatch.
☐ Add a test-owned editable overlay control and prove `ds.type()` and
  `Subject:type()` update observable overlay state.
☐ Place the pointer over a rendered overlay target and exercise physical left
  `CLICK`, `DOWN`, and `UP` through `ds.mouseInput()`.
☐ Preserve the existing wheel-handled and wheel-fall-through assertions.
☐ Exercise `Subject:text()` directly on an overlay descendant.
☐ Capture the mounted overlay tree with `ds.capture_view_tree()` and validate
  bounded root and descendant content.
☐ Capture bounded screen cells with `ds.capture_screen()` while the overlay is
  rendered.
☐ Exercise root-default `ds.inspect()`.
☐ Exercise `{wait=false}` redraw, prove the immediate generation is unchanged,
  and separately await the later overlay render.
☐ Verify backing screen, overlay lifecycle, pointer/button state, viewport, and
  instrumentation restoration after unmount.

### Phase 3 exit gate

☐ Keyboard, text, physical mouse, capture, inspection, and redraw gaps are
  proven through the real overlay host.
☐ Handled overlay input and unhandled backing-screen fall-through remain
  distinct assertions.
☐ Existing overlay-widget automation remains green.

## Phase 4: Complete-screen command gaps

Goal: close the remaining live command gaps for an owned complete-screen mount.

### Tasks

☐ Exercise root-default `ds.inspect()` and fluent `Subject:inspect()`.
☐ Capture the complete-screen component tree and validate that modal or backing
  screens outside the component root are excluded.
☐ Capture bounded screen cells while the complete screen is active.
☐ Exercise top-level and fluent subject pointer placement.
☐ Exercise top-level and fluent hover over a rendered complete-screen control.
☐ Exercise physical click, down, up, and wheel input at the current pointer
  position.
☐ Prove handled complete-screen input and configured backing-screen
  fall-through separately.
☐ Exercise top-level and fluent default-wait redraw.
☐ Exercise `{wait=false}` redraw and separately observe the later completed
  render.
☐ Run the new cases for both a mounted class and an existing instance where
  class/instance ownership can change cleanup behavior.
☐ Verify child modal, mounted screen, backing screen, pause state, viewport,
  pointer/button state, and instrumentation cleanup.

### Phase 4 exit gate

☐ Inspection, capture, pointer, mouse, and redraw gaps are proven on a real
  complete-screen host.
☐ Component-root captures never absorb backing or modal screen ownership.
☐ Class and instance mounts preserve their existing method and cleanup
  contracts.

## Phase 5: Native source and overlay-source command gaps

Goal: complete command coverage for both sources of a non-owning native-screen
attachment without mutating persistent game state.

### Native source tasks

☐ Capture the default native tree with `ds.capture_view_tree()` and validate
  bounded native root, type, names, state, bounds, and available text.
☐ Repeat native root, lookup, and tree capture with explicit
  `ESubjectSource.NATIVE`.
☐ Capture bounded screen cells while the native screen remains current.
☐ Exercise `Subject:text()` directly on the stable live native text widget.
☐ Exercise native `Subject:move_pointer()` and `Subject:hover()` and prove the
  pointer lies within the widget's rendered bounds.
☐ Exercise native `Subject:input()` and observe normal dispatch through a
  test-owned overlay observer without calling it directly.
☐ Exercise native `Subject:click()` and observe normal mouse dispatch through a
  test-owned overlay observer.
☐ Exercise native `Subject:type()` through the same observer so no persistent
  base-game text field is modified.
☐ Exercise native `Subject:redraw({wait=false})` and separately await the later
  render.
☐ Call `ds.viewport()` and assert the explicit non-owning-mount rejection
  without screen, layout, focus, stack, or pointer mutation.

### Overlay source tasks

☐ Capture a test-owned registered overlay tree through
  `ESubjectSource.OVERLAY`.
☐ Exercise overlay-source `Subject:hover()` and verify live bounds.
☐ Exercise overlay-source `Subject:click()` and prove its ordinary activation
  callback result.
☐ Exercise overlay-source `Subject:input()` through normal overlay dispatch.
☐ Add a test-owned editable control and exercise overlay-source
  `Subject:type()`.
☐ Exercise overlay-source `Subject:text()` directly.
☐ Exercise overlay-source `Subject:redraw({wait=false})`.

### Shared cleanup tasks

☐ Capture exact native viewscreen, focus, screen stack, pointer/button state,
  render dispatcher, overlay registry/configuration, and relevant game state
  before attachment.
☐ Unmount and independently prove exact restoration of every captured value.
☐ Prove zero owned-screen and native-dismissal evidence.
☐ Prove native unmount leaves the external overlay registered and enabled.
☐ Restore the test-owned overlay registration and configuration through its
  independent cleanup and prove no staged files or registry entries remain.

### Phase 5 exit gate

☐ Every applicable native-source and overlay-source command has direct live
  evidence.
☐ Text, click, and keyboard observations use test-owned overlay instrumentation
  without bypassing normal dispatch.
☐ The borrowed native screen remains exact, current, unowned, and undismissed.
☐ Native and independently owned overlay cleanup both pass.

## Phase 6: Live rejection, staleness, and recovery contracts

Goal: prove the unit-covered failure boundaries that depend on real DFHack
screen or overlay lifecycle.

### Tasks

☐ For each component mount host, reject native/overlay source options and prove
  the current mount remains usable.
☐ Show a test-owned DFHack Lua screen above the native game screen and prove a
  new native attachment rejects the current/DF viewscreen mismatch.
☐ Dismiss only that test-owned screen and prove the original native screen,
  focus, and stack are restored.
☐ While a native attachment is active, show a test-owned screen transition and
  prove retained native subjects become stale before widget dereference or
  input dispatch.
☐ Restore the original screen and prove the stale subject does not silently
  rebind.
☐ Unmount and perform a fresh native attachment to prove remount recovery.
☐ With a test-owned registered overlay subject, prove staleness after:
  - disablement;
  - removal;
  - replacement under the same exact registry name.
☐ Prove a new `ds.get()` is required after overlay replacement.
☐ Prove missing and disabled overlay selections return explicit source-aware
  diagnostics without registry mutation.
☐ Induce one reversible native render-observation failure through test-owned
  instrumentation.
☐ Prove the original failure remains primary and partial render observation,
  subjects, attachment state, pointer/button state, and cleanup entries are
  removed.
☐ Verify the process remains responsive and the original native screen remains
  current after every rejection case.

### Phase 6 exit gate

☐ All live rejection cases leave independently verified clean state.
☐ Native and overlay subjects fail explicitly after real lifecycle changes.
☐ Recovery requires and successfully creates fresh subjects and attachments.
☐ No test dismisses or navigates the borrowed native game screen.

## Phase 7: Full verification and coverage closure

Goal: prove the final live matrix and retain precise evidence.

### Tasks

☐ Re-audit every public top-level command and fluent `Subject` method against
  the applicability matrix.
☐ Link every supported cell to a direct live assertion.
☐ Link every shared cell to the exact tested implementation boundary.
☐ Link every unsupported cell to its explicit live rejection assertion.
☐ Record any genuinely unavailable stable native control as an incomplete
  item; do not substitute an unrelated assertion.
☐ Run focused automation specifications sequentially for ordinary widget,
  overlay widget, complete screen, native screen, and lifecycle failures.
☐ Record each focused command, process state, test counts, and cleanup result.
☐ Run `.\tools\Run-AutomationTests.ps1` and require zero failures, errors, or
  pending tests plus confirmed cleanup.
☐ Run `.\tools\Run-UnitTests.ps1`.
☐ Run `.\tools\Check-Lua.ps1`.
☐ Build the local rock only if production or packaged source changed; record why
  packaging was required or why it was not applicable.
☐ Run `git diff --check` and `git diff --cached --check`.
☐ Review `git status --short` and account for every changed or added file.
☐ Query final DFHack process state and prove the original screen/focus plus zero
  test-owned screens, overlays, files, pointer/button claims, and render hooks.
☐ Update the matrix so no cell remains classified as missing without an
  explicit incomplete reason.

### Phase 7 exit gate

☐ The final applicability matrix contains no unexplained missing cells.
☐ Focused and full live automation pass with independently confirmed cleanup.
☐ Full unit and Lua syntax checks pass.
☐ Conditional package verification, diff hygiene, worktree accounting, and
  final process-state checks pass.
☐ Every earlier phase and exit gate is complete.

